{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 400,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the universe goes on forever, does that\nmean there are infinite versions of you?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And does that mean that one of them didn't do that really embarrassing thing that one time?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14760,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cosmological equations that so beautifully\ndescribe our universe make an uncomfortable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19300,
    "dDurationMs": 4979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prediction: interpreting them in the most\nstraightforward way, they tell us that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24279,
    "dDurationMs": 2171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe may be infinite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26450,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or not; it could turn out that the universe\ncontains enough matter and energy to close"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30960,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in on itself and be finite, or perhaps the\nsimplest interpretation of the cosmological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35090,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equations is too simple."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37750,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But according to our best theoretical understanding,\nan infinite universe seems at least possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43510,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and some would say likely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46140,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If so this raises an even more crazy possibility."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50150,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An infinite universe may literally contain\nevery possible thing allowable by the laws"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55280,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of physics - each in infinite multitude."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59240,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that includes infinite versions of you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60940,
    "dDurationMs": 7910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today I’m going to try to convince THIS\nversion of you that all those other versions are also real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68850,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To start with we need though we need some monkeys."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72220,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, we need infinite monkeys."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75040,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You may have heard the old thought experiment\n- if an infinite number of monkeys tap randomly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on an infinite number of typewriters, eventually\none of them will accidentally type out the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84280,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complete works of Shakespeare."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86500,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This proposition is the infinite monkey theorem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90120,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reasoning is simple enough: assume each\nmonkey is tapping at all keys randomly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95420,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a chance that a given monkey will\ntap the first character of the first play:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99750,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a capital “A”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101250,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say there’s around a one in 100\nchance on a typical typewriter assuming capital \"A\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106130,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is as likely as small \"a.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107900,
    "dDurationMs": 7190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the second character - a space - 1 in 100 again and so on. “A tempestuous noise of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115090,
    "dDurationMs": 9550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thunder and lightning heard...” the chance\nof that is 1/100^50 or 10^-100 - and that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124640,
    "dDurationMs": 5439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "100 billion billion times less likely than\nwinning a raffle draw in which there are as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130079,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many raffle tickets as there are particles\nin the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133879,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Probably that monkey will mess up on the very\nnext character ... but given enough monkeys"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139489,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one will eventually get through the Tempest,\nmove on to the Two Gentlemen of Verona, Merry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144719,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wives, etc, with 99 out of a hundred monkeys\ndropping out at each subsequent character."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151239,
    "dDurationMs": 4271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And 99 out of every hundred monkeys that make\nit to the last character of the last play"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155510,
    "dDurationMs": 3619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will mess up there ... stupid monkeys."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159129,
    "dDurationMs": 5712,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But one of those monkeys will correctly complete\nCymbeline and all of Shakespeare’s First"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164841,
    "dDurationMs": 5868,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Folio with a probability of 1/100^3.75 million."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170709,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s right, I counted all the characters\nin all Shakespeare's plays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174469,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t say I never do anything for you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178239,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is an unthinkably small probability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181769,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s not zero probability, and the Infinite\nMonkey Theorem tells us that any non-zero"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186879,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probability event will definitely happen given\na sufficiently large number of trials."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193519,
    "dDurationMs": 4821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps you can start to see how this applies\nto there being infinite yous in an infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198340,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199340,
    "dDurationMs": 5939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a perfectly deterministic universe, the\nstarting conditions in any given region - like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205279,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "positions, velocities, etc of all particles\n- perfectly determines the future history"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210650,
    "dDurationMs": 2519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of any point with that region."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213169,
    "dDurationMs": 5091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is, until the disrupting influence of\nexternal regions has time to reach that point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218260,
    "dDurationMs": 5349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The properties of each region are effectively\nrandom - set in the beginning of the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223609,
    "dDurationMs": 1541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by quantum processes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225150,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If they’re set just right then the future\nhistory of that region will be identical to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230900,
    "dDurationMs": 5399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our part of the universe, leading to the formation\nof the Milky Way, the Earth, William Shakespeare,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236299,
    "dDurationMs": 1211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237510,
    "dDurationMs": 4809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The probability of getting every particle\njust right is unthinkably smaller than the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242319,
    "dDurationMs": 4521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "already unthinkably small probability of a\nmonkey typing out Shakespeare."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246840,
    "dDurationMs": 5159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s not a zero probability, and so\nwith infinite regions of the universe - infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251999,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trials, the infinite monkey theorem tells\nus it’s got to happen somewhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257049,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, it’s got to happen infinite times\nand with infinite variations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262449,
    "dDurationMs": 3421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So infinite yous and infinite versions of\nyou."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265870,
    "dDurationMs": 4139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is, IF our assumptions are right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270009,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To decide if this is really the right conclusion\nthere are a few things we need to get straight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276169,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, in such an infinite universe we\nwouldn’t find everything - only everything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281889,
    "dDurationMs": 6161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that COULD happen from the range of initial\nconditions developing under the laws of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288050,
    "dDurationMs": 4619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Infinite monkeys with standard English typewriters\nwill eventually reproduce all of Shakespeare"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292669,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and every Harry Potter, but lacking an inverted\nquestion mark could never do the original"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298150,
    "dDurationMs": 3989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spanish version of Borge’s Library of Babel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302139,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Similarly, there’s no version of you out\nthere where you have Captain Marvel superpowers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307289,
    "dDurationMs": 3831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Though I guess there could be one where you’re\nBatman."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311120,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or NOT Batman, if, you know, you’re already\nBatman in this one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314330,
    "dDurationMs": 6179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so refining our question: in an infinite\nuniverse, does every POSSIBLE thing happen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320509,
    "dDurationMs": 1481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite times?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321990,
    "dDurationMs": 2290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well still, not necessarily."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324280,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If there are infinite possible starting configurations\nfor any one region of the universe, then there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329360,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be infinite regions without any doubling\nup."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if there are finite starting points then\nat least SOME of those starting configurations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338080,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have to be repeated infinite times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341160,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Technically we could imagine that OUR part\nof the universe is unique and it’s just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346870,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other regions that are duplicated, but we\nhave no good reason to assign that sort of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351610,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "specialness to our region."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354050,
    "dDurationMs": 8179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if there really are finite possible starting\nconditions then this region is probably repeated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362229,
    "dDurationMs": 6011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what does it mean for two regions of the\nuniverse to have the same starting conditions?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368240,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It means that every particle, or chunk of\nquantum field, or whatever elementary pixel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373000,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of reality - has matching properties between\nthe two regions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377919,
    "dDurationMs": 3391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How closely matching would they need to be?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381310,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Close enough that the subsequent 13 and a\nhalf billion years of bouncing around leads"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386229,
    "dDurationMs": 5131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to indistinguishable results - and to you\nexisting now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391360,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In such a chaotic system, even tiny differences\nin the starting conditions will lead to massive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397250,
    "dDurationMs": 2389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "divergences in that future history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399639,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in principle there is an allowable level\nof ridiculously tiny deviation in the starting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406419,
    "dDurationMs": 6101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conditions between two regions that would\nstill lead near-identical 13.5 billion year"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412520,
    "dDurationMs": 1170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "histories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413690,
    "dDurationMs": 4689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can’t tune the starting conditions to\nan infinite degree and still get different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418379,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "results - and that’s the key point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421509,
    "dDurationMs": 3391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems there must be finite possible starting\nconfigurations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424900,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means at least some regions must be repeated\ninfinitely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430550,
    "dDurationMs": 1589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Therefore you’re Batman."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432139,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should also note that it’s not just the\nparticles that define starting conditions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436919,
    "dDurationMs": 2611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s also the laws of physics themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439530,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The constants of nature or the energy of the\nvacuum may well be different between regions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444569,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in most cases we expect these values to\nrepeat themselves infinite times in an infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449860,
    "dDurationMs": 6179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe - eventually leading to an exact-enough\nrepetition of both the laws of physics AND"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456039,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the arrangement of particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458499,
    "dDurationMs": 4431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Repetitions of the initial conditions are\ninevitable as long as the properties defining"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462930,
    "dDurationMs": 5229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those conditions can take on a finite range\nof values."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468159,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If one or more properties of the universe\ncan take on values over an infinite range"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472699,
    "dDurationMs": 2351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then no repetitions would be necessary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475050,
    "dDurationMs": 5459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For things like the mass or charge or other\nproperties of individual particles, an infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480509,
    "dDurationMs": 4571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "range means going to very, very high values\nfor these properties - and that tends to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485080,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prohibited by a combination of conservation\nlaws and the formation of black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490279,
    "dDurationMs": 5461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It IS technically possible for the energy\nof the vacuum itself to have any value, even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495740,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enormously large."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497410,
    "dDurationMs": 6009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But universes with large vacuum energies will\nexponentially expand, until that vacuum energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503419,
    "dDurationMs": 3951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decays to lower values in smaller regions\nwithin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507370,
    "dDurationMs": 5049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact that’s essentially the eternal inflation\npicture - one of the most popular ways to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512419,
    "dDurationMs": 1941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce infinite universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I simplified this argument a bit, but elaborating\ndoesn’t change the conclusion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520600,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, you could argue that fundamental\nquantum randomness will cause even identical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526170,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "starting configurations to produce different\nresults."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528690,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that still doesn’t give us an infinite\nnumber of distinguishable histories because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534510,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the number of possible configurations of particles\nat every instant is still finite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540350,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, quantum randomness could allow different\nstarting conditions to evolve into a universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545320,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that looks like this one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547000,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s actually a much simpler proof that\nthere’s a finite number of different possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551230,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "configurations of any region of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554410,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jacob Bekenstein and Steven Hawking showed\nthat the maximum amount of entropy in any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559910,
    "dDurationMs": 6730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "region of space is proportional to the number\nof tiny Planck areas covering its surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566660,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can interpret that Bekenstein bound as\nthe maximum number bits of hidden information."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573030,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And 2 to the power of which is the number of\ndifferent possible configurations for that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579060,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "volume."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580380,
    "dDurationMs": 12260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For our 46-billion light year observable universe there are definitely no more than 2^10^123 possible unique configurations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592640,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As long as the greater universe has more than\nthan that many observable universe-sized regions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599170,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of them should be identical to this one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602730,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And actually, the Bekenstein bound is the\nhard upper limit - for non-black holes the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608010,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number of possible configurations is much,\nmuch lower."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610880,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so unless there’s something weird hiding\nin the laws of physics that we don’t understand,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616500,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an infinite universe probably does duplicates\nits parts infinite times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621770,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We covered most of the important assumptions\nin this crazy hypothesis - except the main"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627620,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628620,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That the universe is actually infinite at\nall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631190,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this may never be testable - we can measure\nthe observable part of the universe to greater"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635650,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and greater precision and see if it has an\nopen or closed geometry... but even then we still"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641330,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have to hope that it’s reasonable to extrapolate\nour cosmological equations forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647320,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, if we somehow verify the\neternal inflation picture then it’s very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652740,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard to avoid an infinite universe, or at\nleast one that’s infinite-enough to duplicate itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659860,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other-other hand, many good physicists are\nvery uncomfortable allowing infinities into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665960,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any models describing the physical universe\n- and their intuition shouldn’t be entirely discounted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672060,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s get back to the real question:\nin an infinite universe, is it inevitable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677490,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that somewhere a monkey will type out Shakespeare?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680970,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well actually, the experiment has been done - researchers at the University of Plymouth gave six"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687330,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Celebes crested macaques a computer for one month."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691530,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Turns out monkeys don’t hit all keys randomly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694280,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact mostly these ones hit the letter S\nand pooped on the machine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699310,
    "dDurationMs": 7300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But who knows - perhaps there’s a near duplicate\nregion of the universe where Shakespeare’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706610,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plays are just long strings of S’s underscored\nwith fecal smears - and where the infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711311,
    "dDurationMs": 3209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monkey theorem has been experimentally verified."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714520,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As for whether there are infinite versions\nand variations of you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719690,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Honestly, I was dismissive of the idea before\nI started writing this script."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724380,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I think it’s hard to avoid that conclusion\n... assuming an infinite universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730470,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at risk of over quoting whatshisname: I could\nbe bounded in a nutshell, and count my self"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736550,
    "dDurationMs": 2910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a King of infinite spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739460,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we're catching up on comments for the last\ntwo weeks - first up we got the three-body"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744450,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem and then we'll do our episode on quantum\nhacking with the s-matrix theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749870,
    "dDurationMs": 7690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ilavenya rightly points out that no nice analytical\nsolutions to the 3-body problem exist in general"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757560,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity - Einstein's modern theory of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760760,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The solutions only exist in special cases for Newtonian\ngravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765190,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in fact it's worse than that - forget\nthree bodies, general relativity gives no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770690,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfect analytical solutions for TWO bodies\nin orbit around each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776190,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's because objects in orbit are all moving,\nso the curvature of the background spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781630,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is constantly changing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783510,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's hard to write down a simple equation\nof motion when the coordinate system is changing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789110,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, approximate solutions work very\nwell in some cases - for example if one of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794290,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the masses is much heavier than the other\nthen the Schwarzschild solution gives simple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798890,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equations for orbits which work pretty well\nas long as the bodies aren't too close together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804380,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jordan Miller suggests that the Euler and\nLagrange solutions to the three body problem are just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808780,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2 body solutions in disguise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811920,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So to remind you - Euler and Lagrange came up\nwith a set of 3-body configurations that are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819360,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now known as the Lagrange points."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821680,
    "dDurationMs": 7110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In any 2-body system, there are these 5 places\nthat a third body can be placed and will stay in stable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828790,
    "dDurationMs": 1090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829880,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the 3rd body has a low mass relative to\nthe others then the original 2-body system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is undisturbed, and in that case Jordan is correct. It's a two body solution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839440,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually, if you're willing to adjust\nthe original 2-body system then a 3rd body"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846340,
    "dDurationMs": 7370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of any mass can be added - either with 3 bodies\nin a permanent straight line or in an equalateral"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853710,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "triangle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854850,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These would no longer be called \"Lagrange\npoints\", but they are true stable or metastable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859410,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3-body orbits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860980,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "RobTheImpure would like to know what is meant\nwhen we talk about \"spaceless timeless particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866960,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scattering\" in the context of the s-matrix."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870050,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is indeed a weird idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872330,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How can particles scatter off each other without\nsome notion of cause and effect or location?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877550,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well to start with, there is a causal order for\nthe incoming and outgoing particles - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883300,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "former cause the latter, and so they must come first."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888450,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in s-matrix theory and quantum field\ntheory, time and space in the interaction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894320,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "region are fuzzy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895320,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'll say more on this in followup episodes,\nbut to give you a simple example."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900910,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine an interaction where an electron emits\na virtual photon which then deflects another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906240,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles - say, a proton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908750,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's exactly the same interaction as if\nthe proton emitted the photon to deflect the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913890,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron - in other words, the direction of\nthe flow of time is irrelevant from the perspective"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919610,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the virtual photon - what matters is the\nstructure of the interaction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924270,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another example is the s-channel - t-channel\nduality, in which you get identical scattering"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929090,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "results when space and time switch places."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933150,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And on that note I do have a couple of corrections\nfrom the last two episodes.I got the labeling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938790,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the t and s channels backwards in the S-Matrix episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942680,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The t channel is where 2 particles scatter\noff each other by exchanging a virtual particle,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947190,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the s-channel is where the particles\nannihilate each other into a virtual particle,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952140,
    "dDurationMs": 1790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which then creates 2 new particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953930,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I said them the other way around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956500,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also I mispronounced the name Newton's great\nwork - it's PrinKipia - hard K, not soft principia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962650,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don't you hate it when you read a word a million\ntimes before you hearing it said out loud,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967530,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but by then it's stuck in your head that way\nno matter how many times you get corrected you still say it that way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972980,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, usually I blame mispronunciations\non my dialect - as in \"oh, yeah, that's how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977710,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we say it in Australia\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978710,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I don't think that works in this case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981190,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And finally, there was some interest in our\ndepiction of the proton being made of lego"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985490,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bricks as a viable theory - quantum lego dynamics,\nas Steve Plegge puts it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991580,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "VioletteOrdinaire prefers this offering from\nDenmark it's much more straightforward than the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997870,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other Copenhagen interpretation."
    } ]
  } ]
}
